<CaseStudy
  title="The Paper Trail"
  tagline="How to “automate” report writing, and more."
  description="Case study: context engineering at work, turning scattered client history into governed, agent-readable knowledge, with curated libraries and measured matching."
  current="home"
>
  <div class="tldr" slot="tldr">
    <strong>TL;DR</strong>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Problem:</strong> every report meant re-hunting months of client emails and attachments; the early fixes (prompt-stuffing first, RAG after) produced fluent garbage.</li>
      <li><strong>What it does:</strong> files correspondence at intake, with metadata: who sent it, which project, which report section it feeds. Attachments are indexed when they land in Drive, not when they're needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Why it works:</strong> report sections collect what was addressed to them instead of searching for it. Opening sections run on client history; EoL sections run on standard practice and approved wording only.</li>
      <li><strong>Governance:</strong> new knowledge enters the library through a human review gate, and colleagues operate the system from a written primer.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>
    At my day job, a sustainability-software company, we deliver compliance
    reports for manufacturers, and I wanted to automate the writing of them. A report
    like that runs on context: who said what, which file arrived when, what changed
    scope and why. That context lived where it always lives, in people's inboxes,
    in Drive folders, in someone's memory. Compliance doesn't tolerate that: a number
    nobody can audit is a liability, not a deliverable. And AI can't use knowledge
    that isn't structured.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-false-start" data-toc="The false start">The false start: more context, same failure</h2>
  <p>
    My first AI work was in the RAG days, the retrieval era. The pitch was seductive:
    pipe your documents into a vector store, retrieve the right chunks at query time,
    and the answer surfaces. Then I got to watch the same idea fail in its rawest
    form. A student group built an early report-writing skill on company data we
    provided, and did the <em>manual</em> version of RAG: prompt-stuffing. They fed
    it historical reports as examples and knowledge from everywhere, more is better.
    No vector store, no retrieval step, just volume, straight into the prompt.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the failure was identical. The examples were inconsistent with each other and
    the knowledge contradicted itself, and the output was exactly that: fluent,
    confident, and unreliable. Contradictory context in, contradictory prose out.
    That's when the two snapped into focus as one idea: <strong>RAG is prompt-stuffing
    with a retrieval step in front</strong>, the same bet that volume finds truth,
    with the same failure mode, whether you automate the stuffing or do it by hand.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I simplified everything. The lesson I carried out of that era: <strong>retrieval finds text; it doesn't keep knowledge true.</strong> The job now
    is curation, deciding what's true, current, and allowed, subdividing the problem
    until each piece of context has exactly one job, and building the governance that
    keeps it that way. Deterministic rules before model calls, proposals instead of
    auto-commits, humans holding the approve button, quality measured against a gold
    set instead of asserted. AI drafts, rules decide, humans approve.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-i-tried">What I tried</h2>
  <table class="try-table">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Approach</th><th>The bet</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Prompt-stuffing</td>
        <td>Volume finds truth: feed it every example and all the knowledge, more is better.</td>
        <td>Contradictory context in, fluent garbage out. The model averaged the contradictions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>RAG</td>
        <td>Same bet, but automate the stuffing: vector store, retrieve chunks at query time.</td>
        <td>Same failure with a retrieval step in front. Retrieval finds text; it doesn't keep knowledge true.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>One context for the whole report</td>
        <td>Treat "the report" as a single context problem with one knowledge base.</td>
        <td>The sections fight: the opening needs client history, the EoL sections are poisoned by it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Curated, section-addressed context (what shipped)</strong></td>
        <td>Each piece of context gets exactly one job; material is filed to sections at intake; new knowledge enters through a review gate.</td>
        <td>Boring, auditable, and it works. The report collects what was addressed to it instead of searching.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="two-kinds" data-toc="Two kinds of context">One report, two kinds of context</h2>
  <p>
    The key move was to stop treating "the report" as one context problem. It isn't,
    it's several, and they need different things:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The opening sections run on client project history</strong>, scope, correspondence, decisions. That context genuinely exists, but scattered. Hence the routine below: it builds the structured record those sections will draw on.</li>
    <li><strong>The end-of-life sections run on standard practice, not history.</strong> EoL modeling follows established practice shaped by geographical assumptions and configuration choices, so I automated it with <em>no historical examples at all</em> (the old reports were too unstructured and messy to be worth retrieving anyway). Instead the section is generated from first principles: the calculation engine computes EoL for the project's configuration, and curated, approved text snippets, matched to the report section and the run's settings, placeholders filled from the engine's export, turn that computation into prose. The report writes itself from the calculation, not from old reports.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Now it's all connected: the report-writing layer only ever fetches the information
    relevant to the section and configuration at hand, nothing else enters the
    context, so nothing else can contradict it.
  </p>

