  <h2 id="the-mental-model" data-toc="The mental model">The mental model: dictionaries all the way down</h2>
  <p>
    Step back from the diagram and the whole system is one idea, epitomised: <strong>dictionary lookup, taken seriously.</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The <strong>curated library</strong> is literally a dictionary, canonical entries, synonyms as accepted spellings, don't-suggest pairs as "not this word."</li>
    <li>The <strong>sender allowlists</strong> are a dictionary of who belongs to which project.</li>
    <li>The <strong>report snippets</strong> are a dictionary keyed by section and configuration.</li>
    <li>Even the <strong>matcher</strong> is just looking up a misspelled word, typo tolerance and all.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Which makes the false start easy to diagnose: prompt-stuffing old reports was <em>reading
    without a dictionary</em>, a pile of sentences, no entries, no
    editors, hoping the reader infers the language. Retrieval is reading; curation is
    writing the dictionary. And dictionaries need what mine has: editors (the review
    gate), editions (versioned snapshots), and errata (the corrections loop). That's
    the whole of my context engineering, honestly, build the right dictionaries,
    keep them true, and let everything downstream be lookup.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-routine" data-toc="The routine">The routine: correspondence that files itself, carefully</h2>
  <p>
    The first piece exists to feed the report's opening sections. It's an automated routine
    that reads incoming client email, matches each sender to the right project, and
    writes a <em>proposed</em> entry into that project's structured correspondence
    log, summary, topic tag, link back to source, while a separate script files the
    real attachments into the right folder. Client history stops living in someone's
    inbox and becomes a record the report layer can actually query. The stack is
    deliberately boring: Claude Code on a scheduled task, the Gmail, Google Drive,
    and Notion MCPs, and a plain Apps Script for the bytes. Its design
    principles are the whole point:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mechanical before judgment.</strong> Most steps are plain deterministic lookups, a sender either matches the allowlist or it doesn't. Token spend and mistake risk should track actual complexity, not apply uniformly.</li>
    <li><strong>Propose, never auto-commit.</strong> Every match lands as <code>Proposed</code>; a human confirms before it counts. Ambiguous sender? The routine asks rather than guesses.</li>
    <li><strong>A miss stays a miss.</strong> Unknown senders go to triage, flagged, never fuzzy-matched into the closest-looking project. Silently guessing wrong is worse than asking.</li>
    <li><strong>Least privilege, self-imposed where the platform won't.</strong> Dedicated credentials scoped to the few actions needed, no send, no delete, ever. Where the platform doesn't allow narrow scopes, the routine carries its own explicit allowlist of touchable objects; anything else is a bug, not a guess.</li>
    <li><strong>Heavy bytes never touch the AI.</strong> A 100KB spreadsheet never passes through the model's context just to move between two Google products, attachments move on a separate script, outside the agent entirely.</li>
  </ul>

