  <p>
    The log isn't an archive, <strong>it's a router.</strong> Every transaction is
    documented with structured metadata, project, topic, date, link back to source,
    and an index of which report section it matters to. So the report layer doesn't
    search the log; it collects what's already addressed to the section it's writing.
    And the section index is keyed on the report template itself, the most stable
    dictionary in the system. Attachments get the same treatment: the moment a file
    lands in Drive, its relevant data and indexes are documented alongside the entry,
    so by the time a report is written, every source file is already findable and
    already addressed to its section.
  </p>
  <p>
    A router only works if its keys stay true, so the obvious question: how do you
    stop drift? Three pieces are already load-bearing, the gate (nothing routes
    until a human confirms), the triage queue doubling as a drift sensor (a rising
    share of unmatched mail means the world changed and the dictionaries need an
    editor), and the corrections loop: every confirmed match teaches the sender
    allowlist automatically. The piece not yet built is watching drift over time,
    triage and escalation rates charted, so the dictionaries get an editor before
    they go stale.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-library" data-toc="The library">The library: curated knowledge with a review gate</h2>
  <p>
    The second piece powers the EoL sections. It's the knowledge base behind the EoL
    engine (the <a href="/work/eol-fixer">EoL Fixer</a>), the tool that turned
    end-of-life verification from an 80-hour slog into a 24-hour one. The engine's material
    matcher maps messy free-text material names ("Staal, constructieprofielen") to a
    curated reference library, and the same library holds the approved report snippets
    that turn a run's export into prose. The library is the context-engineering part:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Curation with a gate.</strong> Every entry, families, variants, ~720 synonyms in three languages, don't-suggest pairs, lands as <em>Proposed</em> and only reaches a run after a human sets <em>Approved</em>. LLM-proposed synonyms get the same review as human ones; the source is marked either way.</li>
    <li><strong>Domain rules steer the algorithm.</strong> "Material beats product", an EoL name maps to the material being disposed of, not the application, is knowledge no algorithm can invent. It's encoded as an explicit rule, not hoped for in weights.</li>
    <li><strong>Calibrated honesty.</strong> We replaced high/medium/low confidence scores when they proved uncalibrated ("medium" was less reliable than "low"). Labels now carry measured accuracy from a 389-name gold set: exact matches are right 98–99% of the time on the test set, and the eval says so honestly: truly unseen names score lower (78%), and collecting real ones is part of the loop.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrections become memory, designed, and being built.</strong> The review intake is live for maintainer and LLM proposals; the piece in build is the full learning loop: every run's confirmations, rejections, and substitutions flowing back automatically into pending-review intake, approved rows syncing into the next library version, the gold set growing with real names. The same mistake is not made twice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="the-primer" data-toc="The primer">The primer: teaching the machine's mind to non-engineers</h2>
  <p>
    A knowledge system dies if only its builder can feed it. So I wrote the primer,
    a document that teaches the matching engine's mental model to LCA specialists with
    no computer-science background: what a gazetteer is (a dictionary, which is what
    this whole system is), why one typo is allowed but two aren't, what a gold set is, why uncalibrated confidence is worse than none.
    Every CS term gets an LCA analogy; every design choice names the domain rule
    behind it. The goal: colleagues who know what the machine can and can't see,
    and whose feedback lands as something the matcher can act on, not as bug reports
    that need translating.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-it-stands">Where it stands</h2>
  <p>
    Live, daily, on real client projects, not a demo. The routine runs every morning;
    the library has a review queue, a validator, and versioned snapshots; the EoL
    report path is connected end to end, the report skill only fetches what's
    relevant to the section and configuration it's writing, so nothing contradictory
    can leak in. In build, honestly marked: the background-report writer that will
    consume the correspondence log (the indexing happens now, so it's ready), and the
    library's automatic run-feedback loop. Honest open items stay open items: mail
    without the right label is silently missed (a known gap with a planned fix), and
    meeting recaps still arrive by hand. Context engineering, it turns out, is mostly
    knowing exactly where your edges are.
  </p>

